    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">I need to tell you something the market is getting wrong.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">There's a persistent belief circulating through payment operations teams, consultant slide decks, and even AI-generated guidance that <strong style="font-weight:700;">structured ISO 20022 addressing is harder to implement than hybrid</strong>. That it requires more integration effort, more time, more risk.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">It doesn't. And the institutions choosing hybrid on that basis are paying for the same integration work while getting an inferior outcome.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">Here's why. Both structured and hybrid implementations use the same sidecar architecture pattern — connecting to your existing payment infrastructure via standard API without replacing or modifying core systems. The middleware is identical: MuleSoft, Volante, Finastra connectors. The deployment timeline is the same: <strong style="font-weight:700;">10&#8211;16 weeks.</strong> The integration effort is the same. The only difference is the output format.</p>

