    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">Paris, France. Paris, Texas. Paris, Ontario. Paris, Tennessee. Paris, Kentucky. Twenty-eight cities across the globe share the same name — and when a SWIFT message arrives with &#8220;Paris&#8221; written as plain text in an address field, the system has no programmatic way to determine which one.</p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">This is the geographic disambiguation problem, and it sits at the heart of why structured addressing exists. In hybrid format, the word &#8220;Paris&#8221; is bundled together with the street name in a single free-text line. A human operator can probably work out the right city from context. A sanctions screening engine cannot. And a payment requiring manual intervention costs <strong style="font-weight:700;">$25&#8211;50 per exception.</strong></p>

    <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 14px 0;">In structured format, disambiguation is automatic. The city name and the country code are stored in separate, dedicated fields — machine-readable, auditable, and processed in milliseconds. No ambiguity. No manual review.</p>

    <!-- Callout: Cuba Street -->
    <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:20px 0;">
    <tr>
      <td style="background:#F5F9FF;border:1px solid #D0DAEB;border-left:3px solid #43ADC1;border-radius:4px;padding:16px 20px;">
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#232F3E;margin:0 0 10px 0;"><strong style="font-weight:700;">The sanctions angle is even more revealing.</strong> Consider &#8220;Cuba Street, Wellington&#8221; — a real street in New Zealand's capital. In unstructured screening, this triggers Cuba sanctions alerts because the engine sees the word &#8220;Cuba&#8221; without any geographic context. With structured addressing, the country field clearly reads &#8220;New Zealand.&#8221; No alert. No false positive. No compliance analyst spending 20 minutes clearing noise.</p>
        <p style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;margin:0;color:#444444;">
